Fix: NC_readfileF() always fread()s up to the full 4MB READ_BLOCK_SIZE per
iteration, even when the caller asked for far fewer bytes (e.g. the magic
number sniff in readmagic(), which only needs a handful of bytes but runs
on every single nc_open()). For any file smaller than 4MB this causes the
entire file to be read from disk/NFS just to inspect its header.

Regressed by netcdf-c commit 9c33cfc62cfd0e2067e444212d1a3b370792f7f7
("use malloc to increase read chunk size from 1 KB to 4 MB"), present in
netcdf-c >= 4.9.1 (not in 4.9.0). Reported upstream: see
https://github.com/Unidata/netcdf-c (issue to be filed).

Bounding each fread() to what's still actually needed when `amount` is a
bounded (non-negative) request restores the old small-read behaviour for
callers like readmagic(), while keeping the large 4MB block for the
genuine unbounded full-file-read case (amount < 0) that the original
commit intended to speed up.

--- a/libdispatch/dutil.c
+++ b/libdispatch/dutil.c
@@ -286,7 +286,8 @@
     char *part = (char*) malloc(READ_BLOCK_SIZE);

     while(amount < 0 || red < amount) {
-	size_t count = fread(part, 1, READ_BLOCK_SIZE, stream);
+	size_t requested = (amount >= 0 && (size_t)amount - red < READ_BLOCK_SIZE) ? (size_t)amount - red : READ_BLOCK_SIZE;
+	size_t count = fread(part, 1, requested, stream);
 	if(ferror(stream)) {ret = NC_EIO; goto done;}
 	if(count > 0) ncbytesappendn(content,part,(unsigned long)count);
 	red += count;
